exam: It is given that {a} and {b} together hold {T} {t}, and that, upon the transfer of {g} {t} from {a} to {b}, their holdings become equal. Determine the number of {t} originally held by {a}.
exam: Ascertain the original holding of {a}, whereas the combined holding of {a} and {b} amounts to {T} {t} and a transfer of {g} {t} from {a} to {b} would render the two holdings equal.
exam: Two boxes together contain {T} {t}. Whereas the removal of {g} {t} from the first box to the second would equalise their contents, state the number of {t} initially in the first box.
exam: Compute the larger of two quantities, given that their sum is {T} and that the transfer of {g} from the larger to the smaller renders them equal.
exercise: Grandad passed away and left {a} and {b} {T} old coins between them, not quite fairly: if {a} gives {b} {g} coins, they will have the same number. How many coins did {a} get?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {T} {t} together, but {g} of {a}'s 'fell off the back of a lorry'. When {a} gives those {g} to {b}, they have the same number. How many {t} did {a} have?
